{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "gr-wo",
  "connector": "を",
  "gloss": "what you do it to (the object)",
  "title": "を — what you eat, see, buy…",
  "pattern": "object を verb",
  "explanation": "を (said “o”) marks the thing an action is done to: パンを{食|た}べます — I eat bread. Japanese puts the verb last, so the order is the reverse of English: first what, then the verb.",
  "natural": "In casual speech を is often dropped: コーヒー{飲|の}む？ — want a coffee? With anyone you’re polite to, keep it.",
  "stiff": "{好|す}き, {分|わ}かる and ある don’t take を: コーヒーが{好|す}き, not コーヒーを{好|す}き.",
  "examples": [
